<p>
  A preset is a saved look: a set of adjustments you can put on any photo
  with one click. <strong>Presets</strong> is the first icon on the right
  panel's edge, two overlapping circles above Adjustments. It works in the
  grid and in the editor. Click <strong>Adjustments</strong> to go back to
  the sliders.
</p>

<ul class="lp-article-list">
  <li>
    <strong>Apply a preset.</strong> Select one or more photos and click a
    preset in the list. It applies to every selected photo at once, with no
    confirmation, and the page stays open, so you can try one look after
    another. <kbd>Cmd+Z</kbd> undoes the whole batch in one step. A preset
    carries everything except Optics and Crop.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Find one.</strong> The search field at the top filters every
    list and opens each group that has a match.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Save your own.</strong> Get one photo looking right, then
    choose <strong>Save current as preset&hellip;</strong> at the bottom of
    the page, or press <kbd>Cmd+Shift+N</kbd>, and give it a name. It goes
    into <strong>My Presets</strong>, at the top of the list. Each preset
    there has a <strong>&ctdot;</strong> menu with Rename and Delete.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Lightroom presets</h2>
<p>
  LightPhotos reads Lightroom's <code>.xmp</code> develop presets. There
  are two ways to bring them in.
</p>
<ul class="lp-article-list">
  <li>
    <strong>Import from Lightroom&hellip;</strong> reads the
    <code>.xmp</code> files you pick and adds them to My Presets, so they
    keep working if the files move. This is in the desktop app only.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Add preset folder&hellip;</strong> lists the presets in a
    folder and reads them where they are, with nothing copied. You can add
    as many folders as you like. Each preset group gets its own folded
    header under My Presets: its Lightroom group if it has one, otherwise
    the subfolder it sits in, otherwise the folder's name. Use this for a
    preset pack you keep somewhere, or Lightroom's own presets folder.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Preset folders belong to the open <a href="/docs/catalogs.html">catalog</a>,
  the way Lightroom Classic can keep presets with a catalog. My Presets is
  shared by every catalog on the computer. To take a folder off the list,
  open the <strong>Preset folders</strong> menu at the bottom of the page
  and choose Remove. The files stay on disk.
</p>
<p>
  LightPhotos reads a folder again each time you open the Presets page, so
  presets you add to it show up. A Lightroom adjustment that LightPhotos
  has no slider for is left out: hold the pointer over a folder preset to
  see what it can't carry. Profiles, Looks and files that won't read are
  skipped, and the bottom of the page says how many. A scan goes eight
  folders deep and reads up to 5,000 files per preset folder.
</p>
<p>
  In the web app, the browser may ask again for access to a preset folder
  after a restart. The folder then shows as <strong>Click to allow access
  to &hellip;</strong>; click it and allow.
</p>

<p>
  A preset changes only the instructions stored next to the photo, like
  any other adjustment. Fine-tune from there on the
  <a href="/docs/adjust.html">Adjustments</a> page.
</p>
